    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `run_tool` in `opsbot/calls.py`. It calls `session.call_tool(name, arguments)` on the session and returns what the tool produced:\n\n1. If `result.is_error` is set, it raises `ToolCallFailed(name, message)`, where `message` is the result's text blocks joined with `\"\\n\"`.\n2. Otherwise, if `result.structured_content` is not `None`, it returns that value as-is.\n3. Otherwise it returns the text blocks joined with `\"\\n\"`.\n\nText is only taken from `TextContent` blocks; image and other non-text blocks are skipped. I checked the API against the Context7 docs for the MCP Python SDK v2 and the installed `mcp==2.2.0` source. This version uses snake_case attributes (`is_error`, `structured_content`); the older v1 SDK used `isError` and `structuredContent`.\n\nStep 2 checks `is not None` rather than truthiness on purpose. Newer protocol versions allow any JSON value there, so a valid result like `0` or `[]` would otherwise be dropped in favour of the text. I couldn't test that case: my test server negotiated an older protocol version that only allows JSON objects there, so it refused to send `0`.\n\n**Verification:**\n- `pytest -q`: 3 passed.\n- I also ran a small real MCP server over stdio through `opsbot.connection.open_session`:\n  - Text blocks mixed with an image came back as `'a\\nb'`.\n  - A result with both structured content and text returned the structured dict.\n  - An error result raised `ToolCallFailed` with `tool='fail'` and `message='no such\\nversion'`.\n\n**Cleanup needed:** my attempt to remove the two scratch files from that test was blocked by a permission prompt, so they're still in the project root. Please delete `_e2e_server.py` and `_e2e_check.py`, or approve the `rm` and I'll do it.\n\nNo dependencies were added or changed."
  },
